## 7. Common Failure Modes and Mitigation Strategies

### 7.1 Training-Serving Skew

**Description**: Differences between how features are computed at training time vs. serving time, leading to silent model degradation.

**Root Causes**:
- Different code paths for offline feature computation vs. online feature derivation
- Timing differences (training uses point-in-time snapshot; serving uses real-time)
- Different library versions between training and serving environments

**Mitigations**:
- **Design principle**: Train on logged features (features captured from the serving path)
- **Validation**: Automated comparison of feature distributions between training data and production logs
- **ML Lens integration**: Feature drift detection compares training reference against live serving distributions
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE implements training-serving parity by construction—using the same feature extraction code path and validating consistency as a quality gate.

### 7.2 Data Drift

**Description**: Production data distributions diverge from training data, degrading model performance over time.

**Detection**:
- Population Stability Index (PSI) > 0.2 → significant drift
- Kolmogorov-Smirnov test (p-value < 0.05) → statistical divergence
- Per-feature weighted drift: `weighted_drift_score = Σ (PSI_i × SHAP_importance_i)`

**Mitigations**:
- Continuous monitoring via ML Lens with configurable alert thresholds
- Automated retraining triggers when drift exceeds thresholds
- Reference dataset strategy: rolling window (e.g., last 30 days) as adaptive baseline
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE monitors drift continuously and autonomously initiates retraining when triggers fire (Tier 1 for standard retraining). Distinguishes "benign drift" (input changed, output stable) from "harmful drift" (output degraded) to reduce alert fatigue.

### 7.3 Feature Leakage

**Description**: Information from the target variable "leaks" into features during training, creating artificially inflated offline metrics that don't generalize.

**Detection**:
- Suspiciously high offline metrics (perfect or near-perfect AUC)
- Feature importance showing unexpected features at the top
- Large gap between offline and online performance

**Mitigations**:
- Feature importance validation in pre-experiment checklist
- Temporal validation: ensure train/test splits respect time ordering
- Code review checklist specifically checks for leakage patterns
- Automated checks for features correlated with temporal proximity to label generation
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE runs automated leakage detection as part of the offline evaluation gate. Flags any feature with suspiciously high individual predictive power for human review.

### 7.4 Overfitting to Offline Metrics

**Description**: Model optimizes for offline benchmark without improving real user experience online.

**Detection**:
- Offline metrics improve significantly but online metrics show no lift
- Model performance degrades on out-of-distribution or adversarial inputs
- Declining experiment success rate despite "better" models

**Mitigations**:
- Mandatory online validation (A/B or interleaving) before any production rollout
- Diverse evaluation sets including adversarial/edge cases
- Behavioral metrics (coverage, diversity) alongside predictive metrics
- Regular refresh of golden evaluation datasets
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE tracks the offline-online correlation over time and raises concerns when gap widens. Proposes evaluation dataset refreshes when staleness detected.

### 7.5 Infrastructure Bottlenecks

**Description**: Training or serving infrastructure limits experiment velocity or production reliability.

**Common Patterns**:
- GPU cluster queuing delays (jobs waiting hours to start)
- Memory exhaustion during training (OOM errors)
- Serving endpoint saturation under traffic spikes
- Network issues in distributed training (NCCL failures)

**Mitigations**:
- Cost-aware training orchestration: estimate compute before launching
- Resource right-sizing: profile models and select appropriate instance types
- Auto-scaling for serving endpoints with proper capacity planning
- Graceful degradation: fallback to simpler models under resource pressure
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE proactively identifies resource constraints before they become blockers. Suggests alternative approaches (smaller model, distillation, quantization) when infrastructure limits prevent ideal approaches. Implements retry logic with exponential backoff for transient failures.

### 7.6 Reproducibility Failures

**Description**: Inability to recreate previous experimental results, blocking debugging and iteration.

**Root Causes**:
- Non-deterministic operations (random seeds not set)
- Undocumented environment differences
- Data versioning gaps (training data changed between runs)
- Library version drift

**Mitigations**:
- Mandatory random seed setting and logging
- Complete environment capture (Docker containers, library versions)
- Immutable dataset snapshots with versioning
- ML Lineage Graph for full provenance tracing
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE achieves near-perfect reproducibility by design—every run is fully deterministic with complete environment/data/code versioning. When non-determinism is unavoidable (e.g., GPU floating point), documents expected variance ranges.

### 7.7 Concept Drift

**Description**: The relationship between features and labels changes over time (even if feature distributions are stable), meaning the "right answer" for the same input changes.

**Detection**:
- Low input drift but high output drift
- Business metrics declining despite stable model metrics
- User feedback patterns changing

**Mitigations**:
- Regular retraining cadence (weekly/monthly depending on domain velocity)
- Online evaluation that tracks business metrics, not just model metrics
- A/B testing against a regularly-retrained baseline
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE correlates output drift with business metric changes and proactively recommends retraining cadence adjustments. Maintains a "model freshness calendar" that triggers retraining before expected concept shifts.

### 7.8 Alert Fatigue and Monitoring Blindness

**Description**: Too many false-positive alerts lead to humans ignoring real issues, or too few alerts lead to missing degradation.

**Mitigations**:
- Two-stage alerting: only escalate when both input AND output drift confirmed
- Severity-tiered alerts with different response SLAs
- Regular alert audit: suppress known/expected patterns
- Weighted drift scoring (focus on important features)
- **AI-Specific**: The AI MLE is immune to "fatigue" but must still prioritize. Implements intelligent triage—investigates alerts autonomously and only escalates when confirmed actionable, providing full investigation context to humans.

