## 5. Team Structure & Organizational Fit

### 5.1 Where the MLE Sits

Based on the actual organizational structure:

```
Central AI Organization
├── AI & ML Platform Team (Eng)
│   ├── Feature Store Squad
│   ├── Model Serving Squad (TEAMServe/SageMaker)
│   ├── ML Studio Squad (Orchestration)
│   ├── ML Fabric Squad (GPU Compute)
│   └── Observability Squad (ML Lens)
├── Search & Ranking Team
│   ├── ML Engineers (model development)
│   └── ML Systems Engineers (production)
├── Core Machine Learning (DTI)
│   ├── ML Engineers (applied ML)
│   └── Data Scientists (analytics)
├── AI Catalyst Team
│   ├── ML Engineers (agentic research)
│   └── ML Systems Engineers (Praxis/mini-mle)
├── Conversational AI Team
└── Developer AI / Transformations
    ├── ML Engineers (P30-P50)
    └── ML Systems Engineers
```

Sources: [Machine Learning Platform - Teams Structure](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/1017005145/Machine+Learning+Platform+-+Teams+Structure), [(Re-)Introducing Squads in the AU ML Platform Team](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020c9822f67244f4346b0c1bff98957aa5d/blog/2025/11/04/6042990593)

### 5.2 Role Relationships

| **Role** | **Relationship to AI MLE** | **Interaction Pattern** |
|---|---|---|
| **Human MLE (Senior/Principal)** | Supervisor/delegator — sets research direction, approves architecture changes, makes launch decisions | AI MLE handles execution; Human MLE handles judgment |
| **ML Platform Engineer (MLSE)** | Infrastructure provider — builds the tools the AI MLE uses | AI MLE consumes platform APIs; escalates infra issues |
| **Data Scientist** | Upstream collaborator — defines business problems, provides domain expertise | AI MLE executes on defined ML problems |
| **Backend Engineer** | Downstream consumer — integrates ML models into products | AI MLE delivers model artifacts; Engineer integrates |
| **Product Manager** | Stakeholder — defines success metrics and use cases | AI MLE produces data and recommendations for PM decisions |
| **SRE / Agent Reliability Engineer** | Monitor/overseer — monitors AI MLE behavior, detects drift, ensures policy compliance | New role that monitors the AI employee itself |
| **Policy Architect** | Governance — defines guardrails and autonomy boundaries | Translates requirements into machine-executable policies |

### 5.3 AI-Specific Organizational Roles (New)

The AI Employee Strategy defines new roles that emerge to support AI employees:

- **Agent Reliability Engineer** — monitors for logic drift and unexpected behavior in AI employees
- **Policy Architect** — translates corporate/legal requirements into machine-executable guardrails
- **AI Product Owner** — defines what the AI employee is allowed to do, success metrics, boundaries of autonomy

### 5.4 MLE Role Level Calibration

| **Level** | **Title** | **Years Typical** | **AI Employee Equivalent** |
|---|---|---|---|
| P30 | Associate MLE | 1+ years | Entry-level AI MLE (heavily supervised, narrow scope) |
| P40 | MLE II | 3+ years | Mid-level AI MLE (broader autonomy, standard workflows) |
| P50 | Senior MLE | 5-6+ years | **Target autonomy level for AI MLE** — execution and instrumentation |
| P60 | Principal MLE | 8+ years | Human-only — model design, research direction, architectural strategy |

The Digital MLE is designed to cover the **IC5/IC6 execution surface** — freeing human MLEs to operate at the IC6/IC7 strategic layer.

Source: [Phase 1 Deep-Dive: Machine Learning Engineer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TADT/pages/7026569052/Phase+1+Deep-Dive+Machine+Learning+Engineer+MLE)

