## 2. Required Technical Skills & Competency Levels

### 2.1 Core Technical Skills Matrix

Based on MLE job profiles across P30-P60 and the Phase 1 MLE Deep-Dive talent landscape analysis:

| **Skill Category** | **Specific Technologies** | **Competency Level for AI MLE** | **Justification** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Programming Languages** | Python (primary), Java/Kotlin, SQL, Golang/TypeScript | Expert (Python); Working proficiency (others) | Production-quality code requirement across all role levels |
| **ML/DL Frameworks** | PyTorch (primary), TensorFlow, JAX, HuggingFace Transformers, ONNX | Expert (PyTorch, HuggingFace); Advanced (TensorFlow, JAX) | PyTorch is the dominant framework in ML Fabric; HuggingFace for model serving; ONNX for inference pipelines |
| **MLOps & Experiment Tracking** | MLflow (integrated w/ Databricks), Weights & Biases, Statsig (experimentation) | Expert (MLflow, Statsig); Advanced (W&B) | MLflow is the core experiment tracking; Statsig for A/B experiments |
| **Cloud Platforms** | AWS SageMaker, GCP (GKE/Vertex AI), Kubernetes (EKS, GKE) | Advanced (AWS); Working proficiency (GCP) | SageMaker for model serving; GKE for ML Fabric compute; multi-cloud extending |
| **Data Engineering** | Spark (Databricks), Ray (distributed compute), Databricks Unity Catalog | Advanced (all) | Databricks is the core data platform; Ray for distributed workloads |
| **ML Platform Tools** | ML Studio (orchestration), ML Fabric (GPU compute), Tecton (feature store), TEAMServe (model serving), Tarot/ML Registry | Expert (all platform-specific tools) | These are the actual tools an AI MLE interacts with daily |
| **LLM/GenAI** | Prompt engineering, RAG, fine-tuning, LLMOps, AI Gateway | Advanced | LLM applications are now standard across MLE work |
| **Infrastructure** | Docker, Kubernetes (Kubeflow training operator, PyTorchJob, RayJob), Kueue scheduling | Advanced | ML Fabric is Kubernetes-native |
| **Observability** | Splunk (log aggregation), CloudWatch, model monitoring, drift detection | Advanced | Production monitoring is a core AI MLE responsibility |
| **Version Control & CI/CD** | Bitbucket (code), Bitbucket Pipelines (CI/CD), ML Registry (model versioning) | Expert | Code and model versioning are foundational |

Sources: [P50 - MLE](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/transformations/pages/5428777098/P50+-+MLE), [P40 ML Platform MLSE Job Description Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5916365866/P40+ML+Platform+MLSE+Job+Description+Template), [Phase 1 Deep-Dive: Machine Learning Engineer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TADT/pages/7026569052/Phase+1+Deep-Dive+Machine+Learning+Engineer+MLE)

### 2.2 In-Demand Niche Skills (Industry Context)

From the global talent landscape analysis:
- **Rarest skills (2026):** Agentic AI and LLM evaluation/safety
- **Fast-growing:** AIOps
- **Senior-critical:** AI Safety & Ethics
- **Dominant market driver:** LLMs & Generative AI
- **ML role distribution:** Applied ML (67%), Generalist (19%), Research (11%), Infrastructure (3%)

### 2.3 AI-Specific Competencies (Beyond Human MLE)

For an AI employee MLE specifically:

| **AI Employee Competency** | **Description** | **Why It Differs from Human** |
|---|---|---|
| **Policy adherence** | Strict compliance with 3-tier autonomy model (🟢/🟡/🔴) | Must never exceed explicitly permitted actions |
| **Reasoning trace generation** | Produces auditable decision traces for every action | Transparency requirement for trust model |
| **Progressive context management** | Operates within ORBIT context packs without context overload | Uses progressive disclosure rather than full knowledge |
| **Harness execution** | Submits real jobs via ML Fabric CLI, monitors builds, adjusts iteratively | Must operate real tooling, not simulate |
| **Escalation precision** | Knows exactly when to escalate vs. act autonomously | Hard-coded policy boundaries, not judgment calls |
| **Continuous operation** | Operates on scheduled cadence without human invocation | Proactive, not reactive |

